    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Teacher agency and self-direction are key in a teacher’s sustained journey of professional development. However, teachers who are not aware of areas of their teaching who would most benefit from their professional development cannot pursue relevant and meaningful learning goals. Based on teachers’ feedback, existing forms of teacher evaluation in schools do not provide adequate feedback on teachers’ areas of learning needs, but rather, serve largely as mechanisms for performance-related evaluations that detract from the ideal goal of facilitating continuous improvement in teachers’ learning. To address this challenge, we based the design of our tool on Brookfield’s (2009) recommendations for facilitating self-directed learning. ‘Chalk up’ envisions a mobile-responsive application that facilitates a structured process for secondary school teachers in Singapore to develop greater awareness of their learning needs and set actionable plans for learning. Teachers complete a self-assessment of their general pedagogical knowledge and are presented with a learning profile visualizing their self-perceived strengths and learning needs and personalized recommendations of learning resources. Teachers subsequently learn how to set a SMART action plan for their learning. This tool aims to supplement existing evaluation processes within schools but targets teachers to engage in a more timely and structured process of self-assessment and empower them to engage in self-directed learning.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Kwang, P. and Fong, D. (2024). Chalk Up: A self-learning tool for teachers. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/yq703pm4419. https://doi.org/10.25740/yq703pm4419.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
